/*
# Accordion

Stacked pane headers with one pane open at a time, each pane sliding open and
closed on a spring over its height.

The load-bearing mechanic: a pane's content is **present through the whole open
and close**, revealed and concealed by the pane's own clip as its height
animates. That is what makes the motion read as a pane opening rather than as
content appearing — never an instant pop, never a blank gap.

The open pane is **one attribute on the group** — `value`, the open pane's name
— which is exactly `RadioGroup`'s shape. Panes are
the group's direct children; each names itself (`name`, defaulting to its
`label`) and derives `open` from the group. Content is authored as the Pane's
ordinary children — the Pane stacks its header then the content, and clips.

```declare
Accordion [ x = 20, y = 20, width = 260,
    Pane [ label = "Account", initial = true,
        Text [ x = 12, y = 8, width = 230, wrap = true,
            text = "Panes slide on a spring; the content rides the whole way." ]
        ],
    Pane [ label = "Privacy",
        Text [ x = 12, y = 8, width = 230, text = "One pane open at a time." ]
        ]
    ]
```

## value
The **open pane's `name`** — the group's whole value, exactly `RadioGroup`'s shape.
Assign it to open a pane from code; `""` closes all of them. A `Pane` never stores
whether it is open, it derives it from here, which is why the one-open-at-a-time
invariant cannot break.

## label
The pane header's text, and — unless you set `name` — its identity in the group.

## name
This pane's identity, defaulting to its `label`. **Set it when two panes share a label,
or when the label is user-facing copy you expect to change**, so the group's `value`
does not move under you when the wording does.

## initial
Seeds the group: the first pane declaring `initial = true` opens if nothing else has
claimed `value` yet. A one-shot at init, not a binding — **to drive which pane is open
from state, constrain the group's `value` instead**.

## open
Whether this pane is the group's current one. Derived; read it to style your content.

## twist
The header chevron's angle, in radians, sprung between 0 and −π/2. **It turns in step
with the height animation rather than snapping**, which is the whole reason it is a
number and not a swap between two marks — a number can be sprung.

## tabOrder()
Contributes the pane's contents to keyboard traversal only while it is open. A closed
pane is clip-occluded rather than hidden — its content must stay visible for the slide
to carry it — so **this is what stops Tab from descending into a pane the user cannot
see**. The header itself remains a stop either way.

## press()
Opens this pane by writing its `name` to the group. Called by a click and by
Space/Enter on the header.

## onClick
Runs `press()` when enabled.

## onInit
Applies `initial`. Fires once per pane.

## width
A pane is as wide as its accordion; only its height is its own.

## opacity
Disabled dims the whole pane, header and content alike, rather than recolouring it — to
the theme's `disabledOpacity`, or 0.45 when the theme states none. A disabled pane
neither opens nor closes; whatever it shows stays as it is.

## theme
The provided design-token record this widget styles off — a **provided value**
(the app provides it once with `App [ theme = { … } ]`; `Control` and the library
widgets read it). Reads `theme.accent` and its kin as typed members; defaults to the
house look. See `Control.theme`.
*/

include [ "control.declare", "icons/core.declare" ]

class Accordion extends View [
    // The design-token record, a provided value: the app provides it once and
    // this widget reads it, so `theme.x` reads below are plain typed members.
    // Default = the house look.

    theme: Theme = { provided("theme", SanFrancisco) },

    value: string = "",

    // one open pane at a time, stacked flush — the sliding heights are the
    // arrangement; the group just stacks whatever heights the springs say

    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 2 ]
    ]


class Pane extends Control [ width = { parent.width }, clip = true,
    label: string = "",
    name:  string = { label },
    initial: boolean = false,                     // seed the open pane: first Pane sets initial = true

    open: boolean = { (parent as Accordion).value == name && name != "" },

    onInit() { if (initial && (parent as Accordion).value == "") (parent as Accordion).value = name },

    // the slide: height springs between the bare header and the content's own
    // wanted extent; `clip = true` is what carries the content through the
    // whole motion — concealed by the clip, never removed

    slide: Spring [ attribute = height, to = { open ? contentHeight : classroot.header.height }, stiffness = 220, damping = 26, epsilon = 0.4 ],

    // a closed pane is clip-occluded, not hidden — its content stays visible
    // so the slide can carry it, but focus must never descend into it (the
    // web's inert rule; ARIA's inactive-panel rule). The traversal protocol
    // handles it: while closed, this Pane contributes no members — the header
    // stop (the Pane itself) remains.

    tabOrder() { return open ? this.tabDefault() : [] },

    press()   { (parent as Accordion).value = name },
    onClick() { if (!disabled) press() },

    opacity = { disabled ? (typeof theme.disabledOpacity == "number" ? theme.disabledOpacity : 0.45) : 1 },

    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 0 ],

    // headers are filled, edgeless — an edge reads as an input field. The one
    // exception is the component channel of the focus model: while this pane
    // holds keyboard focus (Control's focusVisible — the web's :focus-visible
    // rule), a darker-than-fill edge appears. A pointer click focuses silently
    // — the click is its own feedback, no edge.

    // The twisty rotates rather than swapping between two marks, and it does it
    // on a spring — so it turns in step with the pane sliding open instead of
    // snapping while the height animates. `ChevronIcon` takes an `angle` for
    // exactly this: `dir` is the sugar for four resting orientations, the angle
    // underneath is a number, and a number can be sprung.

    twist: number = 0,
    Spring [ attribute = twist, to = { classroot.open ? 0 : -Math.PI / 2 },
        stiffness = 220, damping = 26, epsilon = 0.004 ],

    header: View [ width = { parent.width }, height = 34,
        cornerRadius = { theme.controlRadius },
        fill = { classroot.open || classroot.hot || classroot.down ? theme.controlSelected : theme.control },
        stroke = { classroot.focusVisible ? stroke(1, theme.textMuted) : null },
        chev: ChevronIcon [ x = 11, y = center, iconSize = 14,
            angle = { classroot.twist }, ink = { theme.textMuted } ],
        t: TextLabel [ x = 32, fontSize = 14, fontWeight = { classroot.open ? "semibold" : "medium" },
            textColor = { theme.text }, text = { classroot.label } ]
        ]
    ]
